{
  "title": "01 — EventPoint architecture and browsing",
  "description": "Source-linked interview study tour. Read the highlighted code, then explain it in your own words.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "package.json",
      "line": 4,
      "description": "Start with the monorepo. The apps are deployable applications; packages hold shared types, validation, configuration and database access. Workspaces and Turborepo coordinate development/builds; they do not imply microservices."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/web/src/app/layout.tsx",
      "line": 37,
      "description": "The Next.js root layout composes authentication, admin counts, maintenance guards, header, workspace shell and footer. Explain why shared layout state is separate from each page."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/web/src/app/page.tsx",
      "line": 29,
      "description": "The homepage loads categories, event summaries and spotlight categories. Notice independent reads run together. This server-rendered page is different from an interactive client component."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/web/src/lib/api.ts",
      "line": 157,
      "description": "Follow the frontend REST client. Filters become query parameters; the backend owns search, ordering and pagination. Identify where an API error is handled by the page."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/api/src/express/app.ts",
      "line": 91,
      "description": "This is the actual Express application factory. Locate middleware and route mounting below. Decorated-looking business classes elsewhere do not make this a NestJS server."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/api/src/express/modules/events/events.routes.ts",
      "line": 25,
      "description": "Read the public event routes and their validation. Follow the controller to the event service rather than jumping directly into SQL."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/api/src/modules/events/events.service.ts",
      "line": 457,
      "description": "Search applies validated filters, SQL ranking and pagination, then hydrates results through Prisma. Active ticket prices produce minPrice/maxPrice. Explain the recent bug where related cards lacked this price contract."
    },
    {
      "file": "apps/web/src/components/events/event-card.tsx",
      "line": 7,
      "description": "The shared card renders a summary, not a full event. Missing price is distinct from a genuine zero-price ticket. Discuss stable rendering and responsive layout."
    },
    {
      "file": "packages/database/prisma/schema.prisma",
      "line": 200,
      "description": "Connect the event to category, venue, organizer, ticket types and bookings. Trace these relationships in the schema before reading more feature code."
    }
  ]
}
